{"causes": ["The Hyksos invasion and subsequent establishment of the Fifteenth Dynasty created a divided Egypt, setting the stage for eventual conflict.", "The gradual consolidation of power by the native Egyptian Seventeenth Dynasty in Upper Egypt fostered a nationalist drive for reunification.", "Ahmose I's strategic military campaigns and determination to expel foreign rulers directly challenged Khamudi's authority and existence."], "effects": ["Khamudi's defeat marked the complete expulsion of the Hyksos from Egypt, ending centuries of foreign rule.", "The unification of Egypt under Ahmose I following Khamudi's downfall initiated the prosperous and powerful New Kingdom.", "The victory over Khamudi and the Hyksos established a precedent for Egyptian imperial expansion into the Levant to prevent future invasions."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "khamudi", "sourceName": "Khamudi", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "second-intermediate-period", "targetName": "Second Intermediate Period", "context": "Khamudi's reign brought the Second Intermediate Period of ancient Egypt to its definitive close."}, {"sourceSlug": "ahmose-i", "sourceName": "Ahmose I", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "khamudi", "targetName": "Khamudi", "context": "Ahmose I's military campaigns directly led to the defeat and expulsion of Khamudi and the Hyksos from Egypt around 1600 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "khamudi", "sourceName": "Khamudi", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "war-of-unification-egypt", "targetName": "War of Unification (Egypt)", "context": "Khamudi was a central figure in the final phase of the Egyptian War of Unification against Ahmose I."}, {"sourceSlug": "khamudi", "sourceName": "Khamudi", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "avaris", "targetName": "Avaris", "context": "Khamudi ruled from Avaris, the Hyksos capital, until its capture by Ahmose I around 1550 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "khamudi", "sourceName": "Khamudi", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "apophis", "targetName": "Apophis", "context": "Khamudi succeeded Apophis as the last pharaoh of the Hyksos Fifteenth Dynasty in the mid-16th century BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Avaris, Egypt", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Sharuhen, Canaan", "role": "Final Siege"}, {"name": "Thebes, Egypt", "role": "Rival Capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Khamudi", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "khamudi"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q879157", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khamudi"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Khamudi's defeat marked the decisive end of Hyksos rule, leading directly to the reunification of Egypt under Ahmose I and the establishment of the powerful New Kingdom, a period of unprecedented imperial expansion and cultural zenith.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-23T19:15:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35907798383", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Khamudi was the last Hyksos ruler of the Fifteenth Dynasty of Egypt. Khamudi came to power in 1534 BC or 1541 BC, ruling the northern portion of Egypt from his capital Avaris. His ultimate defeat at the hands of Ahmose I, after a short reign, marks the end of the Second Intermediate Period.", "newValue": "Khamudi, the final Hyksos monarch of the Fifteenth Dynasty, presided over the northern portion of Egypt from his capital Avaris during a pivotal and brief reign, traditionally dated from 1541 or 1534 BCE to 1600 BCE. His rule marked the culmination of the Second Intermediate Period, an era defined by foreign domination and fragmented political authority within Egypt. Khamudi's significance lies not in his accomplishments, but in his role as the antagonist in the ultimate conflict for Egyptian reunification, pitting his Hyksos kingdom against the resurgent native Egyptian forces from Thebes.

His ascension to the throne followed the long and influential reign of Apophis, a period characterized by diplomatic overtures and intermittent conflicts with the Theban Seventeenth Dynasty. Khamudi inherited a kingdom under siege, as Ahmose I, founder of the Eighteenth Dynasty and the New Kingdom, systematically advanced northward. The climactic events of his reign included the siege and capture of Avaris around 1550 BCE, forcing Khamudi to flee. Following the fall of his capital, Ahmose pursued the retreating Hyksos forces into southern Canaan, besieging them for three years at the fortress of Sharuhen, a campaign that definitively dismantled Hyksos power and influence.

Khamudi's ultimate defeat at Sharuhen around 1600 BCE was more than just the end of a dynasty; it signaled the complete expulsion of the Hyksos from Egypt and the permanent reunification of the Two Lands under native Egyptian rule. This victory paved the way for the unparalleled prosperity, imperial expansion, and cultural flourishing of the New Kingdom, transforming Egypt into a dominant power in the ancient Near East. The memory of Khamudi and the Hyksos became a foundational narrative for the New Kingdom pharaohs, who frequently invoked their expulsion as a testament to their divine mandate and strength."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-23T19:15:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35907798383", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1549 BCE"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1549 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The Hyksos invasion and subsequent establishment of the Fifteenth Dynasty created a divided Egypt, setting the stage for eventual conflict.", "The gradual consolidation of power by the native Egyptian Seventeenth Dynasty in Upper Egypt fostered a nationalist drive for reunification.", "Ahmose I's strategic military campaigns and determination to expel foreign rulers directly challenged Khamudi's authority and existence."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-23T19:15:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35907798383", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1600 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1600 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Khamudi's defeat marked the complete expulsion of the Hyksos from Egypt, ending centuries of foreign rule.", "The unification of Eg